module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Descent.FpqcFullFaithful023T
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Descent.QuasiCoherent.StandardAffineFamily

/-!
# Fullness of the canonical descent functor along a family refining a single morphism

Stacks 023S considers a standard fpqc covering `{Spec Aₖ ⟶ T}` of an affine scheme, and its proof
opens by replacing the family with the single morphism `Spec (∏ₖ Aₖ) ⟶ T`.
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Descent/QuasiCoherent/StandardAffineFamily.lean` carries out that
replacement for the *essential surjectivity* half.  This file carries it out for the **fullness**
half, and so reduces `Stacks.Descent.StandardAffineFullness` to its own one-element case.

The replacement is not a sieve refinement, and it runs in the direction opposite to every
refinement lemma available.  The sieve generated by the family is *contained* in the sieve
generated by the single morphism
(`Stacks.Descent.sieve_ofArrows_le_sieve_ofArrows_singleton`), while both Mathlib's
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.full_pullFunctor` and this development's
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.full_toDescentData_of_refinement` carry fullness from
the smaller sieve to the larger one.  What makes the passage possible here is that the family is a
**Zariski open covering of the intermediate scheme**, so the whole of this file is proved in that
generality: for an arbitrary `b : Z ⟶ S` and an arbitrary family `u k : Xf k ⟶ Z` with
`u k ≫ b = g k`, the comparison functor
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctor` from descent data for `b` to descent data
for `g` is full as soon as morphisms descend along `u`.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Descent.singletonPullFunctor`: the comparison functor
  `F.DescentData (fun _ : Unit ↦ b) ⥤ F.DescentData g`, for an arbitrary pseudofunctor `F`.  It is
  `Stacks.Descent.familyPullFunctor` at `F = Stacks.Descent.qcohPseudofunctor`.
* `Stacks.Descent.SingletonCompatible`: the compatibility condition on a morphism between the
  images of two descent data for `b`, written out at the pullback types; see the implementation
  notes for why it has to be written out.
* `Stacks.Descent.SingletonHomExt`: the separatedness hypothesis of
  `Stacks.Descent.full_singletonPullFunctor` — a morphism between objects over `Y` is determined
  by its pullbacks along the morphisms `r : W ⟶ Y` for which two given morphisms `Y ⟶ Z` both
  factor through members of the family after composing with `r`.
* `Stacks.Descent.AffineSingletonFullness`: the one-element case of
  `Stacks.Descent.StandardAffineFullness`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Descent.full_singletonPullFunctor`: **`Stacks.Descent.singletonPullFunctor` is full**,
  for an arbitrary pseudofunctor, given fullness of the canonical descent functor for the family
  `u` over `Z` and the separatedness `Stacks.Descent.SingletonHomExt`.
* `Stacks.Descent.full_toDescentData_of_sieve_eq`: fullness of the canonical descent functor
  depends only on the sieve the family generates.
* `Stacks.Descent.full_familyPullFunctor`: **`Stacks.Descent.familyPullFunctor` is full** for a
  Zariski open covering `u` of `Z`, with no prestack hypothesis.  Together with
  `Stacks.Descent.faithful_familyPullFunctor` this makes it fully faithful.
* `Stacks.Descent.full_qcohToDescentData_of_full_singleton`: **the family-to-singleton reduction
  of fullness.**  If morphisms of descent data for the single morphism `b : Z ⟶ S` all come from
  morphisms of quasi-coherent modules on `S`, and the family `g` factors through `b` by a Zariski
  open covering of `Z`, then the same holds for `g`.
* `Stacks.Descent.standardAffineFullness_of_affineSingletonFullness`: **the fullness half of
  Stacks 023S for a standard covering follows from its one-element case**, i.e.
  `Stacks.Descent.AffineSingletonFullness` implies
  `Stacks.Descent.StandardAffineFullness`.
* `Stacks.Descent.standardAffineFullness_iff_affineSingletonFullness`: the two are in fact
  **equivalent**, the converse being the instance of the general statement at the index type
  `Unit`.  So the reduction neither weakens nor strengthens the hypothesis.

## Implementation notes

**Everything is proved for an abstract pseudofunctor `F` and only then instantiated at
`Stacks.Descent.qcohPseudofunctor`**: crossing between the
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData` world and the `CategoryTheory.Cat` world is a cheap
`rfl` with `F` a variable and a kernel timeout with `F` instantiated.

The one structural obstacle, and the reason for `Stacks.Descent.SingletonCompatible` and for
`Stacks.Descent.comp_conj_eq_comp_conj`, is that
`((Stacks.Descent.singletonPullFunctor F w).obj D).obj k` is a projection of a `def` and is
therefore **not reducible at `implicit` transparency**, although it is definitionally
`(F.map (u k).op.toLoc).toFunctor.obj (D.obj default)`.  A hypothesis or goal mentioning it is not
type-correct at that transparency as soon as anything else in it is spelled the second way, and
then `rw`, `simp` and even `dsimp` refuse to run — `dsimp` reporting "made no progress" with the
"target expression is not type-correct" note.  The devices that work:

* the components of the given morphism of descent data are extracted once, as a family `θ` whose
  type is spelled in the second way, by `obtain ⟨θ, hθ⟩ : ∃ θ, ∀ k, θ k = ψ.hom k`.  After that no
  statement in the file mentions the projection except through `hθ`, which is an equation whose
  type is the second spelling, so rewriting with it is type-correct;
* the compatibility of the given morphism is restated in terms of `θ` as
  `Stacks.Descent.SingletonCompatible`, and `Stacks.Descent.singletonCompatible_of_hom` proves it
  from `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.Hom.comm` by `exact`, which checks at default
  transparency;
* the compatibility condition of
  `Stacks.Descent.toDescentDataHomOfSingletonCompatible` is likewise discharged by `exact`, the
  structure's own goal being spelled with the projections of
  `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.ofObj`.

The base morphism which `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctorObjHom` receives from
the `hom` field of `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctorObj` is `c₁ ≫ g k₁` and
**not** the `q` of the `hom` field: the `cat_disch` that fills its proof obligation solves the
base as a metavariable.  Every use of
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctorObjHom_eq` here is therefore at that base,
and `Stacks.Descent.SingletonCompatible` is stated at it.

Two further points.  `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctor` does not determine its
reindexing `α` from the commutative square it is given, so `α` is pinned explicitly at every call;
without it the elaborator leaves `α` as a metavariable and the `rw` fails to find its own
pattern.  And neither `CategoryTheory.MorphismProperty.RespectsIso @AlgebraicGeometry.Flat` nor
`AlgebraicGeometry.IsZariskiLocalAtSource @AlgebraicGeometry.Flat` is found by `inferInstance`,
so both are supplied as terms, from
`CategoryTheory.MorphismProperty.IsStableUnderBaseChange.respectsIso` and from
`AlgebraicGeometry.HasRingHomProperty.instIsZariskiLocalAtSource`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 023E](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/023E)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 023S](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/023S)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 023T](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/023T)
-/

@[expose] public section

noncomputable section

open AlgebraicGeometry CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Bicategory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite
  TopologicalSpace
open Pseudofunctor.LocallyDiscreteOpToCat

universe t w v' v u' u

namespace Stacks.Descent

section Conjugation

variable {Dc : Type u} [Category.{v} Dc]

/-- **An equation between conjugates.**  If `A` and `B` agree after conjugating the first by one
pair of comparison morphisms and the second by another, then their conjugates by the opposite
pairs agree.  The hypotheses `hm` and `hp` are the two cancellations that make the conjugations
inverse to one another; in the application both come from
`CategoryTheory.Cat.Hom.hom_inv_id_toNatTrans_app`.

This is stated with the comparison morphisms as plain morphisms, and with the two cancellations as
hypotheses, so that it is applied rather than rewritten with; see the implementation notes. -/
theorem comp_conj_eq_comp_conj {a₁ a₂ b₁ b₂ e₁ e₂ d₁ d₂ : Dc} (m₁ : a₁ ⟶ b₁) (n₁ : b₁ ⟶ a₁)
    (m₂ : b₂ ⟶ a₂) (p₁ : e₁ ⟶ d₁) (p₂ : e₂ ⟶ d₂) (q₂ : d₂ ⟶ e₂) (A : b₁ ⟶ b₂) (B : d₁ ⟶ d₂)
    (P : a₂ ⟶ e₂) (Q : a₁ ⟶ e₁) (hm : m₁ ≫ n₁ = 𝟙 a₁) (hp : p₂ ≫ q₂ = 𝟙 e₂)
    (hb : A ≫ m₂ ≫ P ≫ p₂ = (n₁ ≫ Q ≫ p₁) ≫ B) :
    (m₁ ≫ A ≫ m₂) ≫ P = Q ≫ p₁ ≫ B ≫ q₂ := by
  have h2 : m₁ ≫ (A ≫ m₂ ≫ P ≫ p₂) ≫ q₂ = m₁ ≫ ((n₁ ≫ Q ≫ p₁) ≫ B) ≫ q₂ := by rw [hb]
  simp only [Category.assoc, hp, Category.comp_id] at h2
  rw [← Category.assoc m₁ n₁, hm, Category.id_comp] at h2
  simpa only [Category.assoc] using h2

end Conjugation

section PullHomCalculus

variable {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C] {F : Pseudofunctor (LocallyDiscrete Cᵒᵖ) Cat.{v', u'}}

/-- **A morphism between two pullbacks is determined after a further pullback by its
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.LocallyDiscreteOpToCat.pullHom`.** -/
theorem map_eq_of_pullHom_eq {X₁ X₂ : C} {M₁ : F.obj (.mk (op X₁))} {M₂ : F.obj (.mk (op X₂))}
    {Y : C} {f₁ : Y ⟶ X₁} {f₂ : Y ⟶ X₂}
    (A A' : (F.map f₁.op.toLoc).toFunctor.obj M₁ ⟶ (F.map f₂.op.toLoc).toFunctor.obj M₂)
    {Y' : C} (v : Y' ⟶ Y) (d₁ : Y' ⟶ X₁) (d₂ : Y' ⟶ X₂)
    (h₁ : v ≫ f₁ = d₁) (h₂ : v ≫ f₂ = d₂)
    (hyp : pullHom A v d₁ d₂ h₁ h₂ = pullHom A' v d₁ d₂ h₁ h₂) :
    (F.map v.op.toLoc).toFunctor.map A = (F.map v.op.toLoc).toFunctor.map A' := by
  rw [map_eq_pullHom A v d₁ d₂ h₁ h₂, map_eq_pullHom A' v d₁ d₂ h₁ h₂, hyp]

/-- **Pulling back the image of a morphism under a pullback functor** gives the image of that
morphism under the pullback functor of the composite.  This is
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.mapComp'_naturality_2` read through
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.LocallyDiscreteOpToCat.pullHom`. -/
theorem pullHom_map {X₁ : C} {M₁ N₁ : F.obj (.mk (op X₁))} {Y : C} (f₁ : Y ⟶ X₁) (φ : M₁ ⟶ N₁)
    {Y' : C} (g : Y' ⟶ Y) (gf₁ : Y' ⟶ X₁) (hgf₁ : g ≫ f₁ = gf₁) :
    pullHom ((F.map f₁.op.toLoc).toFunctor.map φ) g gf₁ gf₁ hgf₁ hgf₁ =
      (F.map gf₁.op.toLoc).toFunctor.map φ :=
  F.mapComp'_naturality_2 f₁.op.toLoc g.op.toLoc gf₁.op.toLoc
    (by rw [← Quiver.Hom.comp_toLoc, ← op_comp, hgf₁]) φ

end PullHomCalculus

section Singleton

variable {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C] {F : Pseudofunctor (LocallyDiscrete Cᵒᵖ) Cat.{v', u'}}
  {S Z : C} {K : Type t} {Xf : K → C} {b : Z ⟶ S} {u : ∀ k, Xf k ⟶ Z} {g : ∀ k, Xf k ⟶ S}
  (w : ∀ k, u k ≫ b = g k ≫ 𝟙 S)

/-- **The comparison functor from descent data for a single morphism to descent data for a family
refining it.**  For `b : Z ⟶ S` and a family `g k : Xf k ⟶ S` whose members factor through `b` by
`u k : Xf k ⟶ Z`, this is `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctor` at `p = 𝟙 S` and
`p' = u`, for an arbitrary pseudofunctor.  `Stacks.Descent.familyPullFunctor` is its value at
`Stacks.Descent.qcohPseudofunctor`. -/
abbrev singletonPullFunctor (F : Pseudofunctor (LocallyDiscrete Cᵒᵖ) Cat.{v', u'})
    (w : ∀ k, u k ≫ b = g k ≫ 𝟙 S) : F.DescentData (fun _ : Unit ↦ b) ⥤ F.DescentData g :=
  Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctor F (f := fun _ : Unit ↦ b) (p := 𝟙 S)
    (α := fun _ ↦ (default : Unit)) (p' := u) w

end Singleton

section Compat

variable {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C] {F : Pseudofunctor (LocallyDiscrete Cᵒᵖ) Cat.{v', u'}}
  {S Z : C} {K : Type t} {Xf : K → C} {b : Z ⟶ S} {u : ∀ k, Xf k ⟶ Z} {g : ∀ k, Xf k ⟶ S}
  (w : ∀ k, u k ≫ b = g k ≫ 𝟙 S)

include w in
/-- A morphism into `Z` composed with `b` is the morphism to `S` under which a factorisation
through the family is taken. -/
theorem comp_g_eq_of_comp_u_eq {Y : C} (q : Y ⟶ Z) {k : K} (c : Y ⟶ Xf k)
    (h : c ≫ u k = q) (qb : Y ⟶ S) (hqb : q ≫ b = qb) : c ≫ g k = qb := by
  rw [← hqb, ← h, Category.assoc, w k, Category.comp_id]

variable {D₁ D₂ : F.DescentData (fun _ : Unit ↦ b)}
  (θ : ∀ k, (F.map (u k).op.toLoc).toFunctor.obj (D₁.obj default) ⟶
    (F.map (u k).op.toLoc).toFunctor.obj (D₂.obj default))

/-- The compatibility condition on a morphism between the images of two descent data for `b`
under `Stacks.Descent.singletonPullFunctor`, written with
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctorObjHom` and with the two components taken at
the pullback types rather than at the projections of the image descent datum.

Stating the condition this way is what makes the rest of this section work: the type of a
component of a morphism of `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData` mentions the projection
`((Stacks.Descent.singletonPullFunctor F w).obj D₁).obj k`, which is not reducible at `implicit`
transparency, so no `rw`, `simp` or `dsimp` can act on a hypothesis or goal containing it, while
every statement below is an equation whose two sides are spelled with `D₁.obj default`. -/
def SingletonCompatible : Prop :=
  ∀ ⦃Y : C⦄ ⦃k₁ k₂ : K⦄ (c₁ : Y ⟶ Xf k₁) (c₂ : Y ⟶ Xf k₂) (p₂ : c₂ ≫ g k₂ = c₁ ≫ g k₁),
    (F.map c₁.op.toLoc).toFunctor.map (θ k₁) ≫
        Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctorObjHom (α := fun _ ↦ (default : Unit)) (p' := u)
          w D₂ (c₁ ≫ g k₁) c₁ c₂ rfl p₂ =
      Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctorObjHom (α := fun _ ↦ (default : Unit)) (p' := u)
          w D₁ (c₁ ≫ g k₁) c₁ c₂ rfl p₂ ≫
        (F.map c₂.op.toLoc).toFunctor.map (θ k₂)

/-- **The components of a morphism of descent data for the family satisfy
`Stacks.Descent.SingletonCompatible`.**  This is its compatibility condition, read at the
pullback types. -/
theorem singletonCompatible_of_hom (ψ : (singletonPullFunctor F w).obj D₁ ⟶
    (singletonPullFunctor F w).obj D₂) (hθ : ∀ k, θ k = ψ.hom k) :
    SingletonCompatible w θ := by
  intro Y k₁ k₂ c₁ c₂ p₂
  rw [hθ k₁, hθ k₂]
  exact ψ.comm (c₁ ≫ g k₁) c₁ c₂ rfl p₂

/-- **A compatible family of morphisms between the pullbacks to the members of the family is a
morphism of the canonical descent data over `Z`.**  Over an object `Y` mapping to `Z`, the two
legs into the family compose to one and the same morphism `Y ⟶ Z`, so the transition morphism of
the given descent datum which enters
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctorObjHom` is an identity by
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.hom_self`, and what is left is exactly the comparison of
the two factorisations of `Y ⟶ Z`, which is the transition morphism of
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.ofObj`.

The compatibility condition is discharged by `exact` rather than by rewriting the goal: the goal
is the one the structure generates, spelled with the projections of
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.ofObj`, and it is definitionally the hypothesis in its
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.mapComp'` form, which `exact` checks at default transparency while
`dsimp` cannot even start on it. -/
@[simps]
def toDescentDataHomOfSingletonCompatible (hθ : SingletonCompatible w θ) :
    (F.toDescentData u).obj (D₁.obj default) ⟶ (F.toDescentData u).obj (D₂.obj default) where
  hom k := θ k
  comm _ q _ _ c₁ c₂ h₁ h₂ := by
    have p₂ : c₂ ≫ g _ = c₁ ≫ g _ :=
      (comp_g_eq_of_comp_u_eq w q c₂ h₂ _ rfl).trans (comp_g_eq_of_comp_u_eq w q c₁ h₁ _ rfl).symm
    have hq' : (c₁ ≫ g _) ≫ 𝟙 S = q ≫ b := by
      rw [Category.comp_id]; exact comp_g_eq_of_comp_u_eq w q c₁ h₁ _ rfl
    have hb := hθ c₁ c₂ p₂
    rw [Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctorObjHom_eq (α := fun _ ↦ (default : Unit)) (p' := u)
        w D₂ (c₁ ≫ g _) c₁ c₂ (q ≫ b) q q rfl p₂ hq' h₁ h₂,
      Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctorObjHom_eq (α := fun _ ↦ (default : Unit)) (p' := u)
        w D₁ (c₁ ≫ g _) c₁ c₂ (q ≫ b) q q rfl p₂ hq' h₁ h₂,
      D₁.hom_self (q ≫ b) q rfl, D₂.hom_self (q ≫ b) q rfl] at hb
    simp only [Category.id_comp] at hb
    exact hb

end Compat

section Fullness

variable {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C] {F : Pseudofunctor (LocallyDiscrete Cᵒᵖ) Cat.{v', u'}}
  {S Z : C} {K : Type t} {Xf : K → C} {b : Z ⟶ S} (u : ∀ k, Xf k ⟶ Z) {g : ∀ k, Xf k ⟶ S}

variable (F) in
/-- **Separatedness of morphisms with respect to the family, over an arbitrary object carrying two
morphisms to `Z`.**  This is the hypothesis of `Stacks.Descent.full_singletonPullFunctor` about the
family `u` alone: a morphism between objects over `Y` is determined by its pullbacks along the
morphisms `r : W ⟶ Y` for which both `r ≫ h₁` and `r ≫ h₂` factor through members of the family.

It is phrased without `CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.presheafHom` and without any fibre product,
exactly as the hypothesis of
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.faithful_pullFunctor_of_hom_ext` is, and for the same
reason: a statement mentioning the presheaf of morphisms is not type-correct at `implicit`
transparency once it is unfolded. -/
def SingletonHomExt : Prop :=
  ∀ ⦃Y : C⦄ (h₁ h₂ : Y ⟶ Z) (M N : F.obj (.mk (op Y))) (ρ ρ' : M ⟶ N),
    (∀ ⦃W : C⦄ (r : W ⟶ Y) ⦃k₁ k₂ : K⦄ (c₁ : W ⟶ Xf k₁) (c₂ : W ⟶ Xf k₂),
      c₁ ≫ u k₁ = r ≫ h₁ → c₂ ≫ u k₂ = r ≫ h₂ →
      (F.map r.op.toLoc).toFunctor.map ρ = (F.map r.op.toLoc).toFunctor.map ρ') → ρ = ρ'

/-- **The comparison functor from descent data for a single morphism to descent data for a family
refining it is full.**  The two inputs are descent of morphisms along the family `u` over `Z` —
in the form of fullness of the canonical descent functor for `u`, which is where the Zariski stack
condition enters in the application — and the separatedness
`Stacks.Descent.SingletonHomExt`.

A morphism of descent data for the family is, componentwise, a morphism of the canonical descent
data over `Z` by `Stacks.Descent.toDescentDataHomOfSingletonCompatible`, hence comes from a
morphism `φ` of the two underlying objects on `Z`.  That `φ` is compatible with the transition
morphisms of the two descent data for `b` is checked after a pullback along any `r : W ⟶ Y` whose
two composites factor through the family, where it becomes the compatibility of the given
morphism. -/
theorem full_singletonPullFunctor {w : ∀ k, u k ≫ b = g k ≫ 𝟙 S}
    [(F.toDescentData u).Full] (hdet : SingletonHomExt F u) :
    (singletonPullFunctor F w).Full where
  map_surjective {D₁ D₂} ψ := by
    obtain ⟨θ, hθ⟩ : ∃ θ : ∀ k, (F.map (u k).op.toLoc).toFunctor.obj (D₁.obj default) ⟶
        (F.map (u k).op.toLoc).toFunctor.obj (D₂.obj default), ∀ k, θ k = ψ.hom k :=
      ⟨fun k ↦ ψ.hom k, fun _ ↦ rfl⟩
    obtain ⟨φ, hφ⟩ := (F.toDescentData u).map_surjective
      (toDescentDataHomOfSingletonCompatible w θ (singletonCompatible_of_hom w θ ψ hθ))
    have hφθ : ∀ k, (F.map (u k).op.toLoc).toFunctor.map φ = θ k := fun k ↦ by
      have h := congrFun (congrArg Pseudofunctor.DescentData.Hom.hom hφ) k
      exact h
    refine ⟨{ hom := fun _ ↦ φ, comm := ?_ }, ?_⟩
    · intro Y q _ _ f₁ f₂ hf₁ hf₂
      refine hdet f₁ f₂ _ _ _ _ fun W r k₁ k₂ c₁ c₂ hc₁ hc₂ ↦ ?_
      refine map_eq_of_pullHom_eq _ _ r (c₁ ≫ u k₁) (c₂ ≫ u k₂) hc₁.symm hc₂.symm ?_
      rw [pullHom_comp _ _ r (c₁ ≫ u k₁) (c₁ ≫ u k₁) (c₂ ≫ u k₂) hc₁.symm hc₁.symm hc₂.symm,
        pullHom_comp _ _ r (c₁ ≫ u k₁) (c₂ ≫ u k₂) (c₂ ≫ u k₂) hc₁.symm hc₂.symm hc₂.symm,
        pullHom_map f₁ φ r (c₁ ≫ u k₁) hc₁.symm, pullHom_map f₂ φ r (c₂ ≫ u k₂) hc₂.symm,
        D₂.pullHom_hom r q (r ≫ q) rfl f₁ f₂ hf₁ hf₂ (c₁ ≫ u k₁) (c₂ ≫ u k₂) hc₁.symm hc₂.symm,
        D₁.pullHom_hom r q (r ≫ q) rfl f₁ f₂ hf₁ hf₂ (c₁ ≫ u k₁) (c₂ ≫ u k₂) hc₁.symm hc₂.symm,
        ← pullHom_map (u k₁) φ c₁ (c₁ ≫ u k₁) rfl, hφθ k₁,
        ← pullHom_map (u k₂) φ c₂ (c₂ ≫ u k₂) rfl, hφθ k₂]
      have hgq₁ : c₁ ≫ g k₁ = r ≫ q :=
        comp_g_eq_of_comp_u_eq w (r ≫ f₁) c₁ hc₁ (r ≫ q) (by rw [Category.assoc, hf₁])
      have hgq₂ : c₂ ≫ g k₂ = r ≫ q :=
        comp_g_eq_of_comp_u_eq w (r ≫ f₂) c₂ hc₂ (r ≫ q) (by rw [Category.assoc, hf₂])
      have p₂ : c₂ ≫ g k₂ = c₁ ≫ g k₁ := hgq₂.trans hgq₁.symm
      have hq' : (c₁ ≫ g k₁) ≫ 𝟙 S = r ≫ q := by rw [Category.comp_id]; exact hgq₁
      have hb := singletonCompatible_of_hom w θ ψ hθ c₁ c₂ p₂
      rw [Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctorObjHom_eq (α := fun _ ↦ (default : Unit)) (p' := u)
          w D₂ (c₁ ≫ g k₁) c₁ c₂ (r ≫ q) (c₁ ≫ u k₁) (c₂ ≫ u k₂) rfl p₂ hq' rfl rfl,
        Pseudofunctor.DescentData.pullFunctorObjHom_eq (α := fun _ ↦ (default : Unit)) (p' := u)
          w D₁ (c₁ ≫ g k₁) c₁ c₂ (r ≫ q) (c₁ ≫ u k₁) (c₂ ≫ u k₂) rfl p₂ hq' rfl rfl] at hb
      dsimp only [pullHom]
      exact comp_conj_eq_comp_conj _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _
        (Cat.Hom.hom_inv_id_toNatTrans_app _ _) (Cat.Hom.hom_inv_id_toNatTrans_app _ _) hb
    · ext k
      exact (hφθ k).trans (hθ k)

/-- **Fullness of the canonical descent functor only depends on the sieve the family
generates.**  This is Mathlib's
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.exists_equivalence_of_sieve_eq`, read through
`CategoryTheory.Functor.Full.of_iso`. -/
theorem full_toDescentData_of_sieve_eq {ι : Type t} {T : C} {V : ι → C} (f : ∀ i, V i ⟶ T)
    {ι' : Type w} {V' : ι' → C} (f' : ∀ j, V' j ⟶ T)
    (h : Sieve.ofArrows _ f = Sieve.ofArrows _ f') [(F.toDescentData f).Full] :
    (F.toDescentData f').Full := by
  obtain ⟨e, ⟨i⟩⟩ := Pseudofunctor.DescentData.exists_equivalence_of_sieve_eq F f f' h
  have : e.functor.Full := inferInstance
  exact Functor.Full.of_iso i

end Fullness

section Qcoh

variable {S Z : Scheme.{u}} {K : Type v} {X : K → Scheme.{u}} {b : Z ⟶ S}
  {u : ∀ k, X k ⟶ Z} {g : ∀ k, X k ⟶ S}

/-- **The separatedness hypothesis of `Stacks.Descent.full_singletonPullFunctor`, for
quasi-coherent modules and a Zariski covering `u` of `Z`.**  A morphism of quasi-coherent modules
on a scheme `Y` carrying two morphisms `h₁`, `h₂` to `Z` is determined by its pullbacks along the
members of the covering `Stacks.Descent.pairwisePreimageOpen` of `Y`, on each of which `h₁` and
`h₂` factor through members of the family. -/
theorem singletonHomExt_qcohPseudofunctor [∀ k, IsOpenImmersion (u k)]
    (hu : ⨆ k, (u k).opensRange = ⊤) : SingletonHomExt qcohPseudofunctor.{u} u := by
  intro Y h₁ h₂ M N ρ ρ' hρ
  refine qcoh_hom_ext_of_map_eq (fun kl ↦ (pairwisePreimageOpen u h₁ h₂ kl).ι)
    (iSup_opensRange_ι_pairwisePreimageOpen u h₁ h₂ hu) fun kl ↦ ?_
  exact hρ _ (pairwisePreimageFactor₁ u h₁ h₂ kl) (pairwisePreimageFactor₂ u h₁ h₂ kl)
    (pairwisePreimageFactor₁_comp u h₁ h₂ kl) (pairwisePreimageFactor₂_comp u h₁ h₂ kl)

variable (hg : ∀ k, u k ≫ b = g k)

include hg in
/-- **The family-to-singleton comparison functor is full.**  Fullness of the canonical descent
functor of the open covering `u` of `Z` is
`Stacks.Descent.nonempty_fullyFaithful_qcohToDescentData`, and the separatedness is
`Stacks.Descent.singletonHomExt_qcohPseudofunctor`.  Together with
`Stacks.Descent.faithful_familyPullFunctor` this makes `Stacks.Descent.familyPullFunctor` fully
faithful with **no** prestack hypothesis. -/
theorem full_familyPullFunctor [∀ k, IsOpenImmersion (u k)]
    (hu : ⨆ k, (u k).opensRange = ⊤) : (familyPullFunctor b u g hg).Full :=
  have := (nonempty_fullyFaithful_qcohToDescentData (f := u) hu).some.full
  full_singletonPullFunctor u (w := fun k ↦ (hg k).trans (Category.comp_id _).symm)
    (singletonHomExt_qcohPseudofunctor hu)

include hg in
/-- **The family-to-singleton reduction of the fullness of the canonical descent functor.**  If
every morphism of descent data for the single morphism `b : Z ⟶ S` comes from a morphism of
quasi-coherent modules on `S`, and the family `g` factors through `b` by a Zariski open covering
`u` of `Z`, then the same holds for the family `g`.

This is the direction in which the sieve inequality
`Stacks.Descent.sieve_ofArrows_le_sieve_ofArrows_singleton` runs the wrong way for Mathlib's
refinement lemmas, and the direction opposite to
`CategoryTheory.Pseudofunctor.DescentData.full_toDescentData_of_refinement`: the family generates
the *smaller* sieve, and what makes the passage possible is that `u` is a covering for a topology
for which quasi-coherent modules are a stack. -/
theorem full_qcohToDescentData_of_full_singleton [∀ k, IsOpenImmersion (u k)]
    (hu : ⨆ k, (u k).opensRange = ⊤)
    [(qcohPseudofunctor.toDescentData (fun _ : Unit ↦ b)).Full] :
    (qcohPseudofunctor.toDescentData g).Full :=
  have := full_familyPullFunctor hg hu
  Functor.Full.of_iso (toDescentDataCompFamilyPullFunctorIso hg)

end Qcoh

section Reindex

variable {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C]

/-- **A presieve of arrows does not change when the index type is replaced by a surjection onto
it.** -/
theorem presieve_ofArrows_comp_of_surjective {T : C} {ι : Type t} {ι' : Type w} {V : ι → C}
    (f : ∀ i, V i ⟶ T) (e : ι' → ι) (he : Function.Surjective e) :
    Presieve.ofArrows (fun j ↦ V (e j)) (fun j ↦ f (e j)) = Presieve.ofArrows V f := by
  refine le_antisymm ?_ ?_
  · rintro Y p ⟨j⟩
    exact ⟨e j⟩
  · rintro Y p ⟨i⟩
    obtain ⟨j, rfl⟩ := he i
    exact ⟨j⟩

end Reindex

section StandardAffine

/-- **The one-element case of `Stacks.Descent.StandardAffineFullness`**: fullness of the canonical
descent functor of `Stacks.Descent.qcohPseudofunctor` for a *single* morphism from the spectrum of
a ring which is an fpqc covering of an affine scheme.

This is literally `Stacks.Descent.StandardAffineFullness` with the index type taken to be `Unit`,
so it is a special case of it, and the separating instance for the converse is any standard
covering with more than one member. -/
def AffineSingletonFullness : Prop :=
  ∀ (T : Scheme.{u}) (_ : IsAffine T) (B : CommRingCat.{u}) (b : Spec B ⟶ T)
    (_ : Presieve.ofArrows (fun _ : Unit ↦ Spec B) (fun _ ↦ b) ∈ Scheme.fpqcPrecoverage.{u} T),
    (qcohPseudofunctor.toDescentData (fun _ : Unit ↦ b)).Full

/-- **The fullness half of Stacks 023S for a standard covering follows from its one-element
case.**  The family `Spec (A k) ⟶ T` is replaced by the single morphism
`Spec (∏ₖ A k) ⟶ T` through which it factors: the factorisations are the spectra of the
projections of the product, which are open immersions covering `Spec (∏ₖ A k)` by
`Stacks.Descent.iSup_opensRange_specMap_piComponent`, and the single morphism is obtained from
`AlgebraicGeometry.sigmaSpec`, which is an isomorphism because the index type is finite.

The single morphism is flat because flatness is Zariski-local at the source and its restrictions
to the members of that covering are the members of the given family, and surjective because the
given family is jointly surjective; being a morphism to an affine scheme it is quasi-compact, so
its presieve is an fpqc covering.

The index type of `Stacks.Descent.StandardAffineFullness` is in `Type`, while the product of a
family of rings in `CommRingCat.{u}` is formed over an index type in `Type u`, so the family is
first reindexed along `ULift.down`; this changes neither the presieve, by
`Stacks.Descent.presieve_ofArrows_comp_of_surjective`, nor therefore the fullness of the descent
functor, by `Stacks.Descent.full_toDescentData_of_sieve_eq`. -/
theorem standardAffineFullness_of_affineSingletonFullness (h : AffineSingletonFullness.{u}) :
    StandardAffineFullness.{u} := by
  intro T hT K hK A g hmem
  have := hT
  have := hK
  have hpre : Presieve.ofArrows (fun k' : ULift.{u} K ↦ Spec (A k'.down))
      (fun k' ↦ g k'.down) = Presieve.ofArrows (fun k ↦ Spec (A k)) g :=
    presieve_ofArrows_comp_of_surjective g ULift.down ULift.down_surjective
  have hmem' : Presieve.ofArrows (fun k' : ULift.{u} K ↦ Spec (A k'.down))
      (fun k' ↦ g k'.down) ∈ Scheme.fpqcPrecoverage.{u} T := hpre ▸ hmem
  obtain ⟨hsurj', hflat'⟩ := (Scheme.ofArrows_mem_precoverage_iff _).1
    (Scheme.propQCPrecoverage_le_precoverage (P := @Flat) _ hmem')
  set A' : ULift.{u} K → CommRingCat.{u} := fun k' ↦ A k'.down with hA'
  set B : CommRingCat.{u} := CommRingCat.of (∀ k', (A' k' : Type u)) with hB
  set b : Spec B ⟶ T := inv (sigmaSpec A') ≫ Sigma.desc (fun k' ↦ g k'.down) with hbdef
  have hb : ∀ k', Spec.map (piComponent A' k') ≫ b = g k'.down := fun k' ↦ by
    rw [hbdef, ← ι_sigmaSpec A' k', Category.assoc, IsIso.hom_inv_id_assoc, Sigma.ι_comp_desc]
  have hloc : IsZariskiLocalAtSource @Flat :=
    HasRingHomProperty.instIsZariskiLocalAtSource (Q := RingHom.Flat)
  have hriso : MorphismProperty.RespectsIso @Flat :=
    MorphismProperty.IsStableUnderBaseChange.respectsIso
  have hflatb : Flat b := by
    refine IsZariskiLocalAtSource.of_iSup_eq_top
      (fun k' ↦ (Spec.map (piComponent A' k')).opensRange)
      (iSup_opensRange_specMap_piComponent A') fun k' ↦ ?_
    rw [← IsOpenImmersion.isoOfRangeEq_inv_fac (Spec.map (piComponent A' k'))
      (Scheme.Opens.ι (Spec.map (piComponent A' k')).opensRange)
      (by rw [Scheme.Opens.range_ι, Scheme.Hom.coe_opensRange]), Category.assoc,
      MorphismProperty.cancel_left_of_respectsIso (P := @Flat) _ _, hb k']
    exact hflat' k'
  have hsurjb : Surjective b := by
    refine ⟨fun t ↦ ?_⟩
    obtain ⟨k', y, hy⟩ := hsurj' t
    refine ⟨(Spec.map (piComponent A' k')).base y, ?_⟩
    rw [← Scheme.Hom.comp_apply, hb k']
    exact hy
  have hmemb : Presieve.ofArrows (fun _ : Unit ↦ Spec B) (fun _ ↦ b) ∈
      Scheme.fpqcPrecoverage.{u} T := by
    rw [Presieve.ofArrows_pUnit]
    exact b.singleton_mem_fpqcPrecoverage
  have := h T ‹_› B b hmemb
  have hfull' : (qcohPseudofunctor.toDescentData (fun k' : ULift.{u} K ↦ g k'.down)).Full :=
    full_qcohToDescentData_of_full_singleton hb (iSup_opensRange_specMap_piComponent A')
  exact full_toDescentData_of_sieve_eq _ g (congrArg Sieve.generate hpre)

/-- **The one-element case is a case of the general one.**  Take the index type to be `Unit`.
Together with `Stacks.Descent.standardAffineFullness_of_affineSingletonFullness` this shows that
the two conditions are *equivalent*, so the reduction carried out here weakens nothing and
strengthens nothing. -/
theorem affineSingletonFullness_of_standardAffineFullness (h : StandardAffineFullness.{u}) :
    AffineSingletonFullness.{u} :=
  fun T hT B b hmem ↦ h T hT Unit inferInstance (fun _ ↦ B) (fun _ ↦ b) hmem

/-- **The fullness half of Stacks 023S for standard coverings is equivalent to its one-element
case.** -/
theorem standardAffineFullness_iff_affineSingletonFullness :
    StandardAffineFullness.{u} ↔ AffineSingletonFullness.{u} :=
  ⟨affineSingletonFullness_of_standardAffineFullness,
    standardAffineFullness_of_affineSingletonFullness⟩

end StandardAffine

end Stacks.Descent

